repository-code: "https://github.com/pantaleonfassbender-coder/leadership-geopolitics"
abstract: >-
  A research apparatus of twelve public-domain texts on statecraft, the first
  globalization and military strategy, read as an executive curriculum for
  current leadership and geopolitical challenges. The corpus runs in three
  lines: statecraft (Thucydides, Kautilya, Machiavelli, Bacon, Hobbes), trade
  (Grotius, Mun, Smith) and strategy (Sun Tzu, Clausewitz, Mahan, Mackinder),
  from c. 500 BC to Mackinder's "The Geographical Pivot of History" (1904).
  Each work is represented by its analytically load-bearing passages, 93
  verbatim excerpt units in United States public-domain editions with stable
  paragraph-level citation anchors, and carries a scholarly headnote and an
  executive lens. An introductory essay sets out the case for learning from
  the past and its limits (Koselleck; Neustadt and May; Khong) and derives a
  protocol for responsible historical analogy. With an executive concordance
  mapping eight present-day challenges to the passages, a reproducible term
  co-occurrence atlas, a timeline of the corpus in three lines and three eras,
  and links to two companion tools that apply the method to current events.
  A static site with no build step, cookies or third-party requests.
